A1. Camel cargo. ~ ARAB
A5. Upholstered pants. ~ CHAPS
A10. Because she considered her Adam's apple, she had to give up this. ~ EDEN
A14. What trained nurses take in cases. ~ CARE
A15. A drop on the market for suckers. ~ LEMON
A16. A man trap. ~ LURE
A17. This helped make a name for Epictetus. ~ EPIC
A18. Nose fodder. ~ AROMA
A19. A Minor consideration. ~ ASIA
A20. These can be driven but never led. ~ STAKES
A22. A lady in waiting. ~ SPINSTER
A24. How to put an end to morons. ~ ONS
A25. Nickel parking place. ~ SLOT
A26. How you spend your vacation. ~ SPENDING
A31. According to contract, these have the lead, know the score, and usually get a good hand. ~ DIVAS
A35. What Andy finds handy. ~ MIN
A36. A civil engineer couldn't be this. ~ CURT
A38. A skinny old thing that often goes nude. ~ SCALP
A39. Chaucerian flop houses, ~ INNS
A41. This fellow is always kidding people. ~ STORK
A43. There's many a mash made here. ~ SILO
A44. Old rakes. ~ ROUES
A46. This starts at the tail end. ~ SPIN
A47. This man could have put his wife in a cellar and shaken her. ~ LOT
A48. You have to have a knack for this. ~ KNICK
A49. These should live on the Thousand "I" lands. ~ EGOTISTS
A52. Another word for rips. Page Webster. ~ RITS
A55. Proletariat dobbs. ~ CAP
A56. What we all hope to meet in the sweet buy and buy. ~ EXPENSES
A60. These go with a bread line. ~ KNEADS
A64. A foxy manoeuver. ~ TROT
A65. About the only way apartments are being let in New York. ~ ALONE
A67. How to cool hot dogs. ~ CLIP
A68. A gay young haymaker. ~ RAKE
A69. This isn't raw but it's more than rare. ~ RARER
A70. Wandering in the wet. ~ ASEA
A71. Well, here's lookin' at you! ~ EYES
A72. Standing room only here. ~ SHEDS
A73. This puts you out right at the start. ~ COST

D1. Sky scrapers. ~ ACES
D2. How a lady feels in a new ermine wrap. ~ RAPT
D3. This needs a little stick to give it punch. ~ ARIA
D4. To finger wave. ~ BECKON
D5. Upper stories. ~ CLASSICS
D6. Almost a hero but a trifle too feminine. ~ HER
D7. He's not nearly as black as he's painted. ~ AMOS
D8. A big display (plur.). ~ POMPS
D9. A sluggish liver. ~ SNAIL
D10. These have snappy comebacks. ~ ELASTICS
D11. What Barney Oldfield gave the motorcycle cop. ~ DUST
D12. This keeps close to shore in New York. ~ ERIE
D13. This beer is far off. ~ NEAR
D21. Often found dead on the road. ~ END
D23. People are often recognized by these. ~ NODS
D26. This will keep you from getting down in the mouth. ~ SMIRK
D27. You may pine for this nut in New Mexico, but beware of appendiseedus. ~ PINON
D28. What ails Lazy Daisy. ~ ENNUI
D29. A lot of hard cracks. ~ NUTS
D30. You must put out your hand when you do this. ~ GROPE
D32. Two girls were given a life sentence when they took these. (var. sp.). ~ VAILS
D33. How to make the Great Divide. ~ ALLOT
D34. The laundry sent everything back but these. ~ SPOTS
D37. Triangle affairs. (abbr.). ~ TRIG
D40. Hoards. ~ SECRETES
D42. Brass things crashers don't use to horn in. ~ KNOCKERS
D45. This covers a multitude of shins. ~ SKIN
D50. A burning ambition. ~ TAN
D51. A good thing that can't be kept down. ~ IPECAC
D53. Ain't goin' to reign no more. (plur.). ~ TSARS
D54. Famous "last word." ~ SELAH
D56. They say this is to be in Paris. ~ ETRE
D57. An interior desecrator. ~ XRAY
D58. You're playing with fire when you do this. ~ POKE
D59. The farmer cut his corn because it was this. ~ SORE
D61. Too. ~ ALSO
D62. Stops ticking. ~ DIES
D63. Swanky dog blanket. ~ SPAT
D66. What the children raised in the back yard. ~ NED


